ething is connected back to languages  that existed a hundred thousand years  ago in in some ways that we don't really  know so I to me language is a living  thing and very much connected with the  culture of the people who speak that  language today Russian I I love my  Russian law of novels I love listening  to my Russians my the people that I  listen to argue on my favorite Russian  website are chemists Greek Chinese  culture ji was the other day I was at a  Japanese restaurant and experiencing  their culture and because I speak the  language I feel much more that I can  participate in that culture then if I  went to you know then at the Persian  restaurant where I don't speak the  language so to me if I'm gonna go to the  trouble of learning a language I want to  participate in this culture living  culture Esperanto doesn't offer any of  that  I mean Latin I'm almost more motivated  to learn Latin because Latin is  connected to you know all of the romance  languages that we have today so  Esperanto was created by some idealist  some intellectuals who arbitrarily  decided that they were gonna take this  that and the other from certain  languages of course they didn't include  Asian languages that include African  languages and that this was going to  become an international language so to  me it's artificial so I can't get myself  motivated to learn it but if someone  else for whatever reason wants to learn  Esperanto  please go to it I you know in all of my  life in all the countries that I have  been to I have yet to meet one person  who speaks Esperanto so I'm not very  motivated but I know that there's a  whole community of people who are very  you know enthusiastic and fine and and  one day maybe we'll have Esperanto at  length and and so where this connects  with with the issue of French Immersion  is that in learning a language it's the  key is the learner it's not the teacher  it's the learner and the learner has to  be motivated that's essential and so  