ally  different approach to language learning  if we let kids learn any line on any but  a variety of languages as kit as in  their first or second or third or fourth  year that say when they're 12 or 13 if  they decided now I want to learn French  now I wanna learn Spanish they would  learn it very quickly so in other words  if we have a sit where if the motivation  is there the kids will learn very very  quickly they don't have to suffer  through 12 years of doing geography and  history and French which after all is a  foreign language for them and I can't  help but believe that they would be more  interested in the subject if they could  read it in their own language so there  is a sacrifice so if we allowed kids if  the first and I've mentioned this before  the first three four years five years of  schooling were devoted to allowing the  kids to try any language you know you  don't have to have a teacher of Chinese  or or or Spanish or French there's so  many resources available on the Internet  the teacher just has to show the  learners where they can find audio-video  books help them use the resources that  are on the internet so that they can  read through a text that's in a  different language help them in a  variety of ways don't force them to  study this language or that language and  then let them decide what they want to  learn now of course for political  reasons in countries like Canada we that  you have to learn French but in fact 99%  of English speakers are made are 95% and  are not speaking French so we don't  really achieve anything we're far better  to say do you want to learn Spanish  learn Spanish  why learn Chinese learn Chinese and then  at some point if you want to work for  the federal government are you going to  learn French and if you've learned  another language it won't be so  difficult to learn French so if I  summarize all of this what I'm saying is  that the approach to language learning  should be that it should be fun we want  to 